Original commit body from 9522355b6bfe60fbb0edd9db14833a297c86959e:

Swift's Process row listed launchPath (a property, never a call site),
waitUntilExit, terminate and interrupt, and none of the calls that start
the child. try p.run(), launch(), Process.run(_:arguments:) and
Process.launchedProcess(...) produced no subprocess chain, and the one
chain a launching program got was its wait (INV-vamif). They are now
rowed, and ObjC gains the class-method launchers
launchedTaskWithLaunchPath:arguments: and
launchedTaskWithExecutableURL:arguments:error:terminationHandler:.

terminate / interrupt / suspend / resume signal a child that is already
running, so they move from subprocess to ipc_send in both languages, the
dated WI-dupok ruling python.yaml and rust.yaml already apply (INV-babiz).
waitUntilExit returns nothing (python's multiprocessing.Process.join, not
rowed) and is declared not I/O in new Process / NSTask completeness grants.
HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES follows: Process.launchPath out, the launchers in;
the moved members leave the subprocess exemption set.

Measured on three Swift repos with a pinned-dev A/B: swift-argument-parser
4 chains and SwiftLint 2 move from Process.waitUntilExit to the Process.run
in the same function, SwiftLint gains 2 launches in Docs.swift that had no
chain at all, vapor is unchanged. No new chain lands on anything but
Foundation.Process.

Addresses INV-vamif, INV-babiz.

